next question hey David will coronavirus  finally be the catalyst we need for  people to do mass call outs of work  large-scale strikes coordinated mortgage  and rent defaults and all of those other  protests and activism techniques that  you've been talking about beautiful  question I have talked about those  techniques before I've said many many  times signing petitions online okay  calling your elected officials great but  that in order to really change the  system from the point of view of an  activist you may have to be willing to  participate in some other types of  protest and this includes as the  question asks large scale strikes mask  all-out strategic defaults etcetera the  question of whether coronavirus will  cause that to happen really comes down  to one element and that's how long does  this all last and the reason I say that  is that for a lot of people the $1200  stimulus is not going to be enough  people on unemployment will be able to  sort of remain afloat treading water  assuming that this doesn't last that  much longer than what the models suggest  and most people and this is a feature of  the system not a bug from the purposes  of corporate the corporate hock recei as  soon as things start to open back up  people are going to have to go back to  work okay this is how the system is  designed so I often say to you one of  the impediments to mass rallies to  change the system is that the system  makes it so that people have to keep  going to work people would potentially  lose health insurance if they bail on  their job the system is insulated from  being changed by how it sort of controls  the people that are working within the  system so in the same way right now a  lot of people are staying home  and people understand particularly on  the left that you've got to stay home to  stop the spread of the virus so right  now you're not going to see those mass  rallies because people are doing the  right thing once things start to open up  the people who have been staying home  are also likely to understand the  importance of getting back to work to  feed their families and to get caught up  with rent or mortgages or whatever the  case may be so the system has a way of  perpetuating itself by always making it  difficult and inconvenient and very  risky to actually go out and do that  type of protesting now as far as you  know formal rent strikes for example a  coordinated rent strike would be large  swaths of people say we are not paying  rent on this day or we are not paying  our mortgages on this day a strategic  mortgage default there's no doubt that  if it comes down to you pay your rent or  you buy food or you buy medication I  would expect that rent comes after food  and medication of course every situation  is different depends what is the  medication I get it but generally  speaking that is probably the case but  remember evictions have been slowed down  or stopped in many areas there are  places that have done rent and mortgage  deferrals now importantly that's not  forgiveness a lot of people I hope are  understanding but I fear that some  people are not when you talk about a  rent deferral for three months that  means you don't pay April 1 May 1 June 1  rent but on July 1 you owe all four  months now maybe it'll be phased in  maybe you've got to make up half of each  of the deferred months per month through  the end of the year or something like  that still a lot of it is up in the air  but those deferrals are only deferrals  it is not forgiveness and that is going  to be a problem on the tail end for a  lot of people if you needed to defer 1  months of rent are we likely to imagine  that you're going to be able to afford 4  months of rent on July 1st it doesn't  strike me that way but again when it  comes to the system I don't know that  you will see as many rent in strategic  mortgage defaults  because evictions and foreclosures have  been slowed down or stopped in many  parts of  country and again 